643 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 244 0 R/T(Raman Raman Asha )/E(Raman Raman Asha )/Pg 26 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

644 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 244 0 R/T(Divya Karthik Divya )/E(Divya Karthik Divya )/Pg 26 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

645 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 244 0 R/T(E1 E2 E2 )/E(E1 E2 E2 )/Pg 26 0 R/K[17]>>
endobj

646 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 244 0 R/T(Binary projections: LC = {\(Raman, Divya\), \(Raman, Karthik\), \(Asha, Divya\)}, CE = {\(Divya, E1\), \(Karthik, E2\), \(Divya, E2\)}, LE = {\(Raman, E1\), \(Raman, E2\), \(Asha, E2\)}. Join LC with CE on curator, then with LE on \(lender, exhibition\). The result has four tuples: t1, t2, t3 and the extra tuple \(Raman, Divya, E2\). That tuple was never in T. It says Divya handled a Raman loan in E2, which never happened, and it would wrongly create a custody period and a condition-report duty. So the three-way fact cannot be rebuilt from binary relationships, and the ternary relationship cannot simply be split. )/E(Binary projections: LC = {\(Raman, Divya\), \(Raman, Karthik\), \(Asha, Divya\)}, CE = {\(Divya, E1\), \(Karthik, E2\), \(Divya, E2\)}, LE = {\(Raman, E1\), \(Raman, E2\), \(Asha, E2\)}. Join LC with CE on curator, then with LE on \(lender, exhibition\). The result has four tuples: t1, t2, t3 and the extra tuple \(Raman, Divya, E2\). That tuple was never in T. It says Divya handled a Raman loan in E2, which never happened, and it would wrongly create a custody period and a condition-report duty. So the three-way fact cannot be rebuilt from binary relationships, and the ternary relationship cannot simply be split. )/Pg 26 0 R/K[18]>>
endobj

