1729 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 289 0 R/T(At 100,000 lenders the generated database holds 750,000 artifacts, 150,000 loans, 442,363 loan lines and 375,939 condition reports. Counts grew 1,000 times from N = 100 to N = 100,000 \(loans from 148 to 150,000\), and the three slowest queries \(Q2, Q5 and Q7\) grew by a factor of roughly 4,000 to 7,000, a little worse than linear, as expected for join-heavy queries. For evidence queries this means Q2 \(loaned artifacts with no report\) takes about 1.4 s, which is acceptable for a nightly check but not for a screen that refreshes. Practical steps are a composite index \(artifact_id, loan_id\) on loan_object, a flag or summary table for "has report", or running Q5 per exhibition instead of for the whole museum. Primary keys, foreign keys and the weak-entity design need no change at this size. )/E(At 100,000 lenders the generated database holds 750,000 artifacts, 150,000 loans, 442,363 loan lines and 375,939 condition reports. Counts grew 1,000 times from N = 100 to N = 100,000 \(loans from 148 to 150,000\), and the three slowest queries \(Q2, Q5 and Q7\) grew by a factor of roughly 4,000 to 7,000, a little worse than linear, as expected for join-heavy queries. For evidence queries this means Q2 \(loaned artifacts with no report\) takes about 1.4 s, which is acceptable for a nightly check but not for a screen that refreshes. Practical steps are a composite index \(artifact_id, loan_id\) on loan_object, a flag or summary table for "has report", or running Q5 per exhibition instead of for the whole museum. Primary keys, foreign keys and the weak-entity design need no change at this size. )/Pg 71 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

